this is the first in a series of  tutorials on the cloud HPC toolkit a new  product from Google Cloud that automates  the provisioning of HPC environments  following our best practices it is an  open source product available on GitHub  and is extensible using other open  source tools such as terraform Packer  and ansible  the toolkit features built-in  Integrations for common Services needed  in HPC environments for example we  support file systems including our  managed NFS service file store Intel  deos and ddn luster we support  schedulers such as Cloud patch slarm and  HD condor  core and Community maintained modules  are available and documented in our  GitHub repository  we'll walk through a tutorial that  Provisions a small HPC cluster using  slurm and our managed NFS service file  store  you can complete this tutorial yourself  in your own project on Google Cloud  we estimate that it will take 25 minutes  to complete  we'll work in Cloud shell a command line  environment that allows you to manage  your Cloud resources  the tutorial will show you how to launch  Cloud shell and will provision the  environment for you  click Start to begin the tutorial select  a project and the tutorial will  automatically enable the services  necessary for your HPC environment  click show me to highlight the cloud  shell icon you may be shown a prompt if  this is your first time using cloudshell  you can safely acknowledge this prompt  next we will enable the default compute  engine service account and ensure that  it has the Privileges it needs for slim  to operate  authorize Cloud shell to use your  account credentials to perform this  operation  next we will download the HPC toolkit to  your Cloud shell environment and compile  it this step may take several minutes as  dependencies are downloaded for the  first time  verify that the toolkit built by  printing its version number your version  may be more recent than that shown in  the video  the toolkit creates environments from  blueprints in yaml format  blueprints describe your entire working  HPC environment including infrastructure  components like networks and VMS as well  as the installation and configuration of  software components like your HPC  applications  our example blueprints include a link to  the toolkit modules documentation on  GitHub  the functionality of each module is  briefly described and includes extensive  technical documentation on input  parameters and their behavior  the first step is to create a deployment  using the toolkit this will print  instructions to the screen to deploy  your infrastructure using standard  terraform commands this step initializes  your terraform directory by downloading  plugins and modules  this step examines your Cloud  infrastructure and determines which  resources need to be created  in this case we are starting from  scratch so all the resources need to be  added you can approve the creation by  entering yes  the provisioning will take about five  minutes  once your infrastructure has been  provisioned you can log into it using  the cloud console web interface for  compute engine  it will show you a list of VMS including  the slurm login and controller nodes you  can click SSH next to the login node  if you see a message saying that slurm  is still being configured you should log  out wait a few minutes and reconnect to  the VM at the command line we can submit  a basic job which will test Auto scaling  of compute nodes  three new nodes have been created to run  the jobs that we submitted it will take  about one minute for the nodes to join  the cluster in this case our jobs are  just printing the hostname of the  machine that they ran on  after the job's complete nodes scale  back down to zero to reduce costs  after the nodes have scaled down you can  use terraform to destroy the entire  cluster and bring your cost back to zero  thank you  thank you for watching this introduction  to the HPC toolkit  we showed you how to find additional  tutorials and documentation for all  toolkit modules  during the demo we have seen how the  toolkit can simplify the experience of  using terraform to provision an auto  scaling slim cluster that mounts our  managed NFS solution file store  future tutorials will focus on deploying  applications and customizing your own  blueprints  [Music]
 

